        "title": "The great work myth",
        "text": "Work has a terrible reputation. Open almost any newspaper on almost any day and you will read about how work is killing our marriages, causing stress, depriving children of \"quality time\", emptying local communities and making us depressed. Work has become the thing we blame for all our troubles. The case against work is made, in persuasive language, nearly every day. There is only one problem with it: it is nonsense. The truth is that, as far as work is concerned, we have never had it so good.\n\nOne survey shows that four out of ten British workers say they are \"very satisfied\" with their jobs, more than in France, Germany, Italy or Spain. Average earnings have gone up, many firms offer longer maternity leave, a third of firms now offer sabbaticals, and two-thirds let their staff work from home some of the time. The talk about longer working hours also needs to be put in perspective. The average working day has got longer over the last two decades, but by just one minute and forty-two seconds.\n\nDespite all the improvements in work in recent decades, there is still a deep-rooted attitude that happiness lies outside work, and that we are all just waiting for the weekend. The idea that work is basically bad for us has a long history. Karl Marx described workers as being \"alienated\" from the product of their labour. \"What, then, is the alienation of labour?\" he asked. \"First, the fact that labour is external to the worker, that is, it does not belong to his essential being; that in his work he does not affirm himself but denies himself; does not feel content but unhappy; does not develop his physical and mental energy but mortifies his body and ruins his mind.\" However, constant negativity about work condemns us to exactly the kind of work that Marx was trying to free us from 150 years ago. If we accept that work is dull and degrading, a ransom paid for the hostage of our \"free time\", then we are allowing alienation to continue.\n\nWork is becoming too important for it to be of doubtful quality. Work is a community, the place where we meet friends and form relationships, a provider of our social life as well as our working life. One in three of us meets most of our friends through work, two-thirds of us have dated someone at work and, according to a poll by the recruitment consultancy Sanders and Sidney, a quarter of us meet our life partners there. Work is also becoming a more important sign of who we are. Family, class, region and religion are now less reliable signs of identity, and work is filling the gap, which makes it the most important fact about ourselves that we mention when we meet people. \"Work,\" as Albert Einstein said, \"is the only thing that gives substance to life.\"\n\nThe move of work towards the centre of our lives shows how pointless much of the current debate about \"work–life balance\" is. It is true that some people are working longer hours. Yet the idea that this is being forced on us against our will just doesn't hold up. Take the people who work the most hours, more than sixty a week. Surveys show that they are the ones who say they like their jobs the most. This may seem surprising until you remember that people who like doing something may do more of it than people who do not. People who love their jobs admit to having a \"work–life problem\" because they work more hours than they strictly have to. In truth, they are simply made to feel that they have a problem because of the attitudes around them. Of course, this leaves open the question of who keeps things going at home, and of the effect on children in particular. It may be that these people are choosing to put less time and energy into their home life than others think they should, but if they get more out of their work than out of their home, then perhaps this is a clear and valid choice for them to make.\n\nIn the end, our aim must be to start seeing work as an essential part of our life, rather than something added on to it. Theodore Zeldin, an Oxford academic, has the right plan for the future of work: its abolition. But not in the way anti-work campaigners have in mind. \"We should abolish 'work',\" he says. \"By that I mean abolishing the distinction between work and leisure, one of the greatest mistakes of the last century, which allows employers to keep workers in terrible jobs by giving them some leisure time. We should try to be employed in such a way that we don't realise that what we are doing is work.\" Zeldin sets the challenge for work in the twenty-first century. It is indeed time to give up the idea of work as a down payment on life, but before we can do so, all the modern myths about work will have to be exposed: the ones that keep presenting work as naturally exhausting, degrading and harmful. It is time to give work a break."
